Route B patch 0014 — boot preparation becomes ONE call.
=======================================================

ONE CONCEPTUAL CHANGE: `Updater::PrepareNextBootPatch()` replaces the sequence
`ValidateNextBootPatch()` → `NextBootPatchPath()` → `ReportLaunchStart()`.

WHY. The three steps read the candidate pointer at three different times, and on
iOS they were interleaved in the worst possible order. `ConfigureShorebird` calls
`SetBaseSnapshot()`, which resolves the base isolate snapshot and therefore
reached `ResolveIsolateData()` in `runtime/dart_snapshot.cc` — where launch start
was reported — ONE LINE before `ValidateNextBootPatch()` ran. So the launch was
attributed to a candidate that had not been checked yet.

On device (Arm C, iPhone 7 / iOS 15.8.8, release 1.1.0+1) that produced:

    15:21:13.164  Reporting launch start.            <- records next_boot = 2
    15:21:13.165  Verifying patch signature...
    15:21:13.176  Patch signature is invalid
    15:21:13.178  active path: …/patches/1/dlc.vmcode  <- SELECTION CORRECT
    15:21:13.208  Launch success for patch 2         <- ATTRIBUTION WRONG

Patch 2's bytes never executed — no security defect. But `record_boot_success`
promoted the rejected patch and `cleanup_older_than` then deleted patch 1, the
artifact the fallback depended on, so the NEXT launch dropped to the base
release. Full evidence: `selfhost/evidence/p6-signing/ARM_C_EXECUTION_IDENTITY.md`.

WHY NOT JUST MOVE THE CALL. `update()` and the update thread may change
`next_boot_patch` at any moment, so anything that re-reads the pointer after
selection can still disagree with the bytes the VM was handed. Attribution has to
be bound to the RETURNED patch inside one state transition. The updater-side half
of that lives in the updater repo (see
`0014-updater-atomic-prepare-next-boot.patch`).

THE TWO ACCESSORS ARE REMOVED, not left unused. While they exist a future caller
can reassemble the broken ordering without noticing.

WHAT THE GUARD NOW MEANS. Once-per-process protects PREPARATION, and a second
engine gets the FIRST call's path back, cached. Re-preparing would re-attribute
the launch to whatever has since been downloaded; returning an empty string would
silently downgrade a later engine to the base release. Both are pinned by test,
including concurrent callers.

TEST TARGET CHANGE, and it is not incidental. `updater_unittests.cc` was only
ever compiled by `shorebird_unittests`, which does not link on the host build
(`Shorebird_ReadLinkHeader` comes from Shorebird's private Dart fork) — so all 14
of those tests had never EXECUTED. They are added to `shorebird_arming_unittests`,
the slim target `0008` created for exactly this reason, and now run: 21/21 pass,
and removing the once-per-process cache fails 5 of them.

TWO SHELL TESTS KEEP THEIR SUBJECT, NOT THEIR ASSERTION. They used to prove that
creating a Shell reported launch start exactly once. Production no longer reports
from there, so asserting the same thing would be asserting a fiction; they now
prove the ABSENCE — no snapshot-resolution path may re-enter the updater's launch
lifecycle. That is the regression guard against reintroducing this ordering.

Applies to the engine tree (`/Volumes/build/route-b/flutter`), engine commit
619fdad176 on top of 2c7b8c3ea5.

